323 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 242 0 R
/E (The existing literature demonstrates that Retrieval-Augmented Generation \(RAG\) improves the factual accuracy of AI-generated responses, while Virtual Reality \(VR\) enhances visitor engagement and context-aware techniques enable personalized museum experiences. However, most current systems focus primarily on generating responses and provide limited transparency regarding how those responses are produced. Key limitations include the lack of Explainable AI \(XAI\), absence of source attribution, unavailability of confidence estimation, and limited integration of multiple contextual signals into the retrieval process.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
324 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 242 0 R
/E ( Additionally, many existing solutions remain conceptual or prototype-based and have been evaluated using small datasets or limited user studies, restricting their practical applicability and reducing user trust.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
325 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 242 0 R
/E (The proposed Context-Aware Explainable AI Curator for Virtual Museums using Retrieval-Augmented Generation addresses these research gaps by integrating context-aware retrieval, Explainable AI, source attribution, and confidence scoring into a unified end-to-end framework. By enabling users to understand not only what the AI answers but also why the answer was generated and which knowledge source supports it, the proposed system aims to improve transparency, reliability, user trust, and the overall educational experience in virtual museum environments.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
